# --- s017 Book I d.3 q.2 (= Ord. I d.3 p.1 q.3, nn. 108–166)
n("n349","s017-01","vtrum deus sit obiectum naturale adequatum","la","source","= *Ord.* I d. 3 p. 1 q. 3, n. 108 (*utrum Deus sit primum obiectum naturale adaequatum respectu intellectus viatoris*).","verified")
n("n350","s017-02","est opinio Thome","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 110: *una opinio quae dicit quod primum obiectum intellectus nostri est quiditas rei materialis*, rejected in nn. 112ff. Gorris names Thomas, *ST* I q. 12 a. 4 and q. 85 a. 1, both of which make the quiddity of material things the connatural object.","verified")
n("n351","s017-02","1⟨3. q. 9⟩","la","textual","A is stained at the end of the line. Only *quodlibeto. 1* is legible, and the rest is supplied from B. B reads *Hen. quodlib. xv. q. iiij. et quodli. xiij. q. ix*. A's first reference, *15 q. 9*, therefore differs from B's *xv q. iiij*. Henry's view that God is the first object known (implicitly) is in *Summa* a. 24 and the late quodlibets. Neither numbering has been checked against the Leuven edition.","to-verify")
n("n352","s017-03","primum obiectum primitate adequationis est ens inquantum ens","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 137: *dico quod primum obiectum intellectus nostri est ens, quia in ipso concurrit duplex primitas, scilicet communitatis et virtualitatis*. Gorris's *virtutis* stands for Scotus's *virtualitatis*.","verified")
n("n353","s017-03","preterquam in primis differentiis","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 nn. 131–136: being is not included quidditatively in the ultimate differences or in the *passiones entis*. Gorris's *primis differentiis… entis* seems to mean the differences that divide being (the *differentiae ultimae* of the first concepts).","verified")
n("n354","s017-04","Nihil est quod subterfugiat rationem entis","la","source","The phrase is a commonplace that goes back to Avicenna, *Metaph.* I c. 5–6 (being is predicated of everything). Its wording here does not appear in Scotus's text of this question.","to-verify")
n("n355","s017-04","secundum Franciscum denominatiua","la","commentary","Almost certainly Francis of Meyronnes († c. 1328), the leading early Scotist, whose *Conflatus* on Book I d. 3 discusses whether *ens* is predicated of God quidditatively. B confirms *Franci.* The finding is significant: it is the first named Scotist other than Scotus in Gorris's text, and it shows him using the Mayronist tradition. The position ascribed to Francis has not been checked in the *Conflatus*.","to-verify")
n("n356","s017-05","propositiones de per se non conuertuntur","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 134: *passio 'per se secundo modo' praedicatur de subiecto, I Posteriorum… non est e converso 'per se' sed per accidens. Igitur si ista est per se secundo modo 'ens est unum', haec 'unum est ens' non est per se primo modo*. Gorris, like B, writes *per se secundo modo* in the second place, where Scotus has *primo modo*.","verified")
n("n357","s017-01","Metaphysics II","en","source","Arist. *Metaph.* II 1, 993b24–26 (fire, the hottest, is the cause of heat in other things).")
n("n358","s017-04","Francis","en","historical","Francis of Meyronnes OFM, *doctor illuminatus et acutus*, author of the *Conflatus*. See the note on the Latin.")
